From dc0e1e477324ef0ed7b3436a78ef059528df711c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:01:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Scale the direct beam by the sunlit fraction of the
 accumulation window

ERA5 shortwave is a mean over the accumulation window, but terrain shading
was tested once, at the centre of the window: the direct beam was kept or
removed for the whole hour when the sun cleared or dropped behind a ridge
during it. The downscaler now tests the horizon at the solar_substeps
sub-steps of the window (default 6, as for the top-of-atmosphere average)
and scales the direct beam by the sunlit fraction, each sub-step weighted
by the beam it brings to the slope. Sub-step sun positions are geocentric
(solar_geom.sun_geocentric, within 0.01 deg of the SPA); only terrain
shading counts. solar_substeps: 1 keeps one test per window.

- ds_solar.nc records accumulation_window and solar_substeps; older files
  give the window from solar_geometry_time_offset
- physics check direct_in_terrain_shadow: shaded over the whole window
  (previous, current and next time steps)
- tests: geocentric positions against the SPA, convergence to a 60
  sub-step reference behind a 25 deg horizon, open horizon unchanged,
  older solar files

Davos points, January 2020, against the stations: on the 57 hours the sun
crosses the horizon of DAV, RMSE 30.3 -> 20.6 W m-2 and bias -7.9 -> -2.7;
monthly bias -4.4 -> -4.0. WFJ (crest): 15 hours change, RMSE 12.7 ->
14.1, monthly scores unchanged. No measurable cost (300 clusters: 0.80 s
-> 0.79 s).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012FMkEYDUJxvxmd3SbUsJfP
